<p>
Marcin Borkowski (mbork) has a <a href="https://mbork.pl/2026-11-23_Improving_support_for_named_frames_in_Emacs">nice post on how he uses frames in his workflow</a>. The TL;DR is that because he often works on several projects in parallel, he has a separate frame for each project. That makes a lot of sense. You don’t get buffers confused among projects and everything for a project is “in one place”.
</p>
<p>
That brings up the problem of switching among frames. There is, of course, <code>other-frame</code> (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>x</kbd> <kbd>5</kbd> <kbd>o</kbd>) but like <code>other-window</code> it can be a bit clunky. Mbork set out to remedy that by writing a function to switch to a given named frame. That’s sort of like <a href="https://github.com/abo-abo/ace-window">ace-window</a> for frames, except that you use the frame name instead of a label generated on the fly.
</p>
<p>
The first problem is that frame names don’t have to be unique. It wouldn’t be too hard to enforce unique names manually but mbork decided to rewrite <code>set-frame-name</code> to enforce a unique name when naming a frame. It’s pretty simple and should probably be the Emacs default.
</p>
<p>
Once he had a way of ensuring unique frame names, he rewrote <code>select-frame-by-name</code> to select a frame by name and to create a frame with that name if one doesn’t already exist. The result is a very nice workflow for handling several projects that you’re working on at the same time. Take a look at mbork’s post for the details.</p>
